Z||000000||FiXXXerX||12-22-2001||02:51 PM||||If I went and just pulled out the TBI unit and tried starting the car what do you guys think would happen (I don't really understand TBI, but currently there is no Fuel getting to the engine and its stopped up at the TBI unit, and if it was out it would just drop right into the engine....) any serious reprocusions? Flooding? Anything? The car will start if I dump fuel into the carb, but, unless I continue dumping fuel in it will die. So, theoreticly, if the carb just starts dumping a semi small stream of fuel into the engine would it work? Welp, just wonderin, cause I don't have $140 to buy one right now.<P>Adam<P>------------------<BR>-= If the Front wheels don't leave the ground, its still broken =-||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||12-22-2001||03:26 PM||||Again.... it sounds allot like the fuel lines are reveresed. The TBI is a flow thru design. The only things that could posibly stop that:<P>reveresed lines... Fuel Regulator is a one way device.<BR>blocked/crushed lines...<BR>huge amount of dirt...<P>For details on how both the 300 and 700 TBI work. you need the Haynes Fuel Injection book for 86-96 (# 10220) The 300 is also covered in Haynes Fiero book (# 79008)<P>DO NOT try to start the car with the TBI appart. If you must test the way you propose, bump the key into run just long enough to see is fuel flows. Stuff a rag down the TBI to catch the fuel.<P>Also, are you certain you got the right fuel pump? you can't use one from a V6 car.||||reg||1
Z||000002||FiXXXerX||12-22-2001||04:13 PM||||Right, heres the thing, I have it pumping gas, all the way up the line,I have it takeing gas back through the line into the take from the excess gas, and I also got the Emissions line hooked up. But, the TBI unit (Injector) itsn't doing anything. so the fuel just stops there, what I'm thinking is that the TBi unit is broken, and therefor not pumping or doing its thing (Since it does require a power source and does regulate the flow of gas from what it looks like). So the fuel just rebounds and goes back into the tank without depositing and gas into the actual engine. And the ammount of gas going back into the tank is greater than its supposed to be since no gas is being lost in the actual pumping procedure. The fuel pump and sender unit are the proper ones, I took the sender unit right out of an 84 4 cylinder car and it pumps up and out of the right line. It just gets jammed up when it hits the carb/TBI, and nothing spouts out of it. From the looks of a TBI unit and having at least some basic knowledge of a fuel system, the TBI unit which is attached to the bridge on the carb that hangs over the air intake, has to either drop or spray fuel into the intake manifold, this isn't happening, it just recirculates and goes back into the gas tank, causeing a more than average flow of gasoline do to the fact that there was no fuel disipation. I've come down to the conclusion that its either the electrical harness that clips onto the TBI unit itself, or the TBI unit entirely. In any event I'm going to the Junkyard on Weds to pull off the Carburator that is on the 4cyl I got the sender unit off of. And they were driving that car around, so I'm figureing the TBI and carb on that work fine, and hes only gonna charge me $75 for the whole damn thing. (The engine in the car was knocking thats why they didn't keep runnin it or sell it as a whole thing). So I'm just seeing if there is some way between now and Wedsday that I can get the car running without having to tie my brother on the back of the car with the hood open pouring gas into the engine while we drive down the road..... though the thought is amusing.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Adam<P>------------------<BR>-= If the Front wheels don't leave the ground, its still broken =-||||reg||1
