Z||000004||ITB 2M4||12-22-2001||07:18 PM||||You can buy an injector from Discount or AutoZone for about $40.  I had to replace the one on my '84 not too long ago, and it's an easy swap.<BR>Again, if you want me to come take a look at it, since I live right across the river, just e-mail me on how to get there.  I work for the City of Cape Coral on second shift (3pm-11pm), Thurs. through Mon., so it would have to be sometime when I'm not working.<P>------------------<BR>SCCA ITB '84 Fiero 2M4<BR>AOL IM- DGPFiero||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||12-22-2001||07:28 PM||||Ok, then the pump should be ok... the regulator could be failed in what case you have low fuel pressure. pinch the return line to increase fuel pressure. If the regulator is shot then that will tell you quick.<P>Next... DO NOT fart around with trying to jumper 12V or ground to the injector. If you do that wrong you'll likely fry the ECM. By your own posts, you said you didn't understand the system.....<P>Yours is an 84-86 as I remember... Go to a parts store get haynes Fiero book and a "noid" light. Plug the noid light into the harness instead of the injector. crank the motor. The light will turn on or flash as the ECM tries to fire the injector. No light... wire, fuse, or ECM problem. Light? injector or fuel pressure problem.<P>You can also buy noid lights from several of the parts/tools links in my cave. they are small test lights mad specifically for testing EFI systems. A single noid light is pretty cheap. $5-10<P>84-86 4 cyl... you want the noid light for a 300 TBI<BR>87-88 4 cyl... you want the noid light for a 700 TBI<P>Lisle make a universal noid light that has a long cord so you can plant it where it can be seen from inside the car. that one is expensive. <p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 12-22-2001).]||||reg||1
